  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Friday)\nIssued at 1139 AM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nFor the rest of today, isolated storms are drifting northwards.\nBut with weak flow and weak large scale forcing, storm coverage\nwill be limited, which will help lower the overall severe risk. \nEnough instability exists that severe wind gusts and severe hail\nare possible. For now, thinking is these storms drifting\nnorthwards will travel east to northeast this afternoon, tracking\nthrough the SD plains through tonight. Today's afternoon temps\nwill be much above normal, in the 90s to near 100. Overnight temps\nwill cool to the 60s for most of the area, little cooler in the\nBlack Hills.\n\nAround round of passing storms is likely tomorrow/Sunday. An \nupper level trough, currently sitting and spinning over the \npacific northwest, will swing over Montana. This trough will \nlikely be the main weather maker for the area for a couple days. \nAs it kicks east and north, thunderstorms will be likely ahead of\nthe trough. And this kind of pattern has had a history of\nproducing severe weather. The SPC has placed a marginal severe \nrisk out for Sunday over the Black Hills and to the north and \nmachine learning severe weather models are hinting at the risk of\nsevere weather centered over the CWA Sunday into Monday. For now,\nthunderstorms will likely get going around noon over the Black\nHills and those will track northward. Then late Sunday into Sunday\nnight, a reinforcing round of moisture and storms will drift\nnorthward through the area. Now is a good time to be sure of\nhaving a way of being informed of severe weather in your area.\n\nFor next week, very low chances for precip lingers in the \nforecast with slightly cooler weather Tuesday and Wednesday, \nbefore the upper 80s to 90s return later in the week.",
